WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.650
A human body holds thirty trillion cells; an elephant carries a hundred times that, and lives about as long as we do.

2
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:14.020
If every cell division is one more lottery ticket for cancer, elephants should have run out of luck long ago.

3
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:20.870
Yet they die of cancer less often than we do — and we could only understand why once someone read their genome.

4
00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:37.840
In nineteen seventy-seven, an epidemiologist working at Oxford was looking at cancer statistics and asked a question nobody was asking out loud.

5
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:45.810
Richard Peto was at the time working on a model of how cancer arises; a simple and merciless model that still sits in the textbooks today.

6
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.460
Cancer does not begin with a single accident.

7
00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:57.110
For a cell to slip out of control, several faults, several mutations, have to pile up in that same cell.

8
00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:05.560
One error that breaks the growth brake, another that disables the suicide mechanism, a third that cripples the system correcting copying errors.

9
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.090
Each on its own is unlikely.

10
00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:16.860
But if your body holds a very large number of cells, and those cells keep on dividing for a very long time, low probability wins sooner or later.

11
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.510
Now take that model seriously and do the arithmetic.

12
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:24.160
A human being has roughly thirty trillion cells.

13
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:31.970
A mouse carries about a thousand times fewer cells than a human and lives on average two years; a human goes on up to eighty.

14
00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:36.740
Multiply the cell count by a thousand and the lifespan by thirty.

15
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:45.350
If the risk of any given cell turning cancerous were the same from species to species, a human ought to see astronomically more cancer than a mouse.

16
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.320
A difference so large that not one human would ever reach adulthood.

17
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.890
But we do.

18
00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:57.900
That was what Peto noticed: the model is consistent on the inside, and it does not match the world outside.

19
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.390
Scale things up and it gets stranger still.

20
00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.240
Think of an African elephant.

21
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.050
Four, five, sometimes six tonnes.

22
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:13.620
It carries roughly a hundred times more cells than a human and lives sixty, seventy years in the wild.

23
00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:19.350
So it has a human's long life, with a hundred times a human's cells.

24
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:26.520
Run the same arithmetic for the elephant and this is what falls out: no elephant should ever reach adolescence.

25
00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:35.290
It ought to be impossible for an old female to lead the herd to water on forty years of memory, because that female should long since have died with dozens of tumours.

26
00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:43.580
Move on to the blue whale, look at more than a hundred tonnes of mass and a lifespan beyond eighty years, and the arithmetic becomes outright absurd.

27
00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.910
But elephants do not die of cancer.

28
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.920
Not at our rate, at any rate.

29
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:57.890
In two thousand fifteen a team led by Lisa Abegglen and Joshua Schiffman at the University of Utah decided to close the question with numbers.

30
00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.740
They went into zoo necropsy records.

31
00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:09.670
They combed through fourteen years of necropsy data from the San Diego Zoo, along with a separate database covering six hundred and forty-four elephant deaths.

32
00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:14.920
The result was this: about four point eight percent of elephants die of cancer.

33
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:22.250
In humans the same figure runs between eleven and twenty-five percent, depending on which country and which year you look at.

34
00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:30.860
So the animal carrying a hundred times more cells is the animal whose chance of dying from cancer is a third of ours, or even a fifth.

35
00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.790
This is called Peto's paradox, and calling it a paradox is a little unfair.

36
00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.640
There is no contradiction in nature.

37
00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.970
No law of physics breaks in the elephant.

38
00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:46.100
The contradiction is in our heads, or more precisely in our model.

39
00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:51.190
The model assumed that cancer risk per cell is constant from animal to animal.

40
00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:54.360
Nature never signed that assumption.

41
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.010
And that is where the real force of Peto's question lies.

42
00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:07.100
It contains not an answer but a claim: if large animals are not getting as much cancer as they ought to, then evolution must have handed them something extra.

43
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:09.470
A shield.

44
00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:13.920
And that shield, in principle at least, is a findable thing.

45
00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.370
It sits inside the genome; it can be read, it can be counted.

46
00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.540
For thirty-eight years this stayed a thought experiment.

47
00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.710
Then somebody looked at the elephant's genome.

48
00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.400
Where to look was, in fact, obvious.

49
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:33.290
There is one gene in cancer biology that has been nicknamed the guardian of the genome: TP53.

50
00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.740
Its job, described plainly, is unnervingly simple.

51
00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:46.190
When DNA damage appears in a cell, the protein TP53 produces steps in, halts division, and calls in the repair crew.

52
00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:52.720
If the damage is too severe to be repaired, it presses a second button and tells the cell to kill itself.

53
00:04:52.720 --> 00:05:00.050
Eliminating a suspect cell instead of repairing it means cancelling the first step of cancer before that step is even taken.

54
00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:06.100
In humans there is a single copy of this gene; one version from the mother, one from the father.

55
00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:10.710
There is a painful reason we know how critical that single copy is.

56
00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:18.680
There is an inherited condition called Li-Fraumeni syndrome, in which a person is born missing one of the working copies of TP53.

57
00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:22.330
They are left with one functioning version rather than two.

58
00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:26.300
The result is a lifetime cancer risk above ninety percent.

59
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:31.390
The absence of a single version shifts a person's cancer statistics that far.

60
00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:37.440
What the Utah team found in the elephant genome was this: twenty copies of TP53.

61
00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.170
Forty versions.

62
00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.540
Twenty times what we carry.

63
00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:46.150
Finding it is one thing; showing that it works is another.

64
00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:51.720
The team took blood cells from elephants and exposed them to radiation in the laboratory.

65
00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.970
They did the same to healthy human cells and to cells from people with Li-Fraumeni syndrome.

66
00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:03.660
The moment the elephant cells registered the damage, they killed themselves at a far higher rate than the human cells did.

67
00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:09.230
The Li-Fraumeni cells were the most reluctant of all; they carried on living with the damage.

68
00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.600
What emerged looked like a spectrum.

69
00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:20.210
At one end a cell that ignores damage, in the middle ours, and at the far end the elephant cell, which has no tolerance for doubt whatsoever.

70
00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:24.180
The elephant strategy is not to repair but to sacrifice.

71
00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:29.590
The reason it can afford that is entirely practical: the elephant has cells to spare.

72
00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:36.440
An attitude that would count as wastefulness at our scale becomes, at that mass, a sensible insurance policy.

73
00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:44.890
During those same years, in Chicago, Vincent Lynch's team was coming at the problem from another direction, and found a stranger detail.

74
00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:49.180
Nineteen of the elephant's twenty copies are not ordinary gene copies.

75
00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.710
They are called retrogenes.

76
00:06:51.710 --> 00:07:00.960
They form when the processed, message-stage copy of a gene is pasted back into the DNA in reverse; they contain none of the intervening pieces called introns.

77
00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:07.650
In other words, the elephant's genome has photocopied the same document over and over and filed it in different folders.

78
00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:11.940
Lynch's team went a step further and looked at the elephant's relatives.

79
00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:20.230
The rock hyrax and the manatee are small animals sitting on the same evolutionary branch as the elephant, and they have only a handful of these copies.

80
00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:24.680
The number of copies rises as the body grows along the family tree.

81
00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:28.330
This is a little more than correlation.

82
00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:34.700
As the bodies of the elephant's ancestors grew, so did the stock of spare versions protecting that body.

83
00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:42.670
When the team transferred the elephant retrogenes into mouse cells, the mouse cells too began killing themselves more readily in the face of damage.

84
00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:46.480
So the copies are not decorative; they work.

85
00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:53.970
Then in two thousand eighteen, with a study published in the journal Cell Reports, came the most cinematic part of the story.

86
00:07:53.970 --> 00:08:00.500
Lynch's team found a large number of copies of a gene called LIF in the elephant genome; between seven and eleven of them.

87
00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:04.470
Almost all of them are pseudogenes, which is to say dead.

88
00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:09.240
Broken texts with the key snapped off, no longer read.

89
00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:13.210
Genomes are full of graveyards like this; we have them too.

90
00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:18.140
But one of them, the copy named LIF6, has climbed out of the grave.

91
00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:24.670
Right at the head of that copy, a piece of DNA that shifted position millions of years ago has stuck on a switch.

92
00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:28.960
And the thing that turns the switch is precisely TP53.

93
00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:43.490
The circuit runs like this: DNA damage occurs in the cell, TP53 wakes up, TP53 goes and switches on LIF6, and the protein LIF6 produces travels to the cell's mitochondria and punches straight through them.

94
00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:47.940
Once mitochondria are punctured, there is no way back for the cell.

95
00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:52.710
The researchers called it a zombie gene, because that is exactly what it is.

96
00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:56.360
Dead once, then returned in a different job.

97
00:08:56.360 --> 00:09:02.090
The elephant took a piece of scrap left over from its ancestors and turned it into a murder weapon.

98
00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:06.060
It should be added that this is not the only solution.

99
00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:11.150
Nature has solved the same problem in entirely different ways in other species.

100
00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:19.120
The naked mole rat weighs all of thirty-five grams and lives more than thirty years, which is extraordinary for a rodent that size.

101
00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:22.610
And cancer is almost never seen in it.

102
00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:32.180
As Vera Gorbunova and Andrei Seluanov at the University of Rochester showed in two thousand thirteen, this animal's secret is not in TP53 copies.

103
00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:37.590
It lies in a molecule called hyaluronan that fills the spaces between its tissues.

104
00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:42.840
The hyaluronan the mole rat produces is more than five times larger than ours.

105
00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:52.090
That oversized molecule makes the cells hypersensitive; the instant cells touch one another they stop dividing, which means they never permit the crowding a tumour needs.

106
00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:58.780
When the researchers silenced the gene that produces this molecule, the animal's cells became prone to cancer.

107
00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:05.950
In two thousand twenty-three the same team transferred that gene into mice; cancer went down in the mice and lifespan went up.

108
00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:08.640
So there is no single formula.

109
00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:13.730
The elephant hoarded copies; the naked mole rat packed gel between its cells.

110
00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:18.020
Two separate lineages found two separate answers to the same bill.

111
00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:23.270
Everything I have described so far took a serious shaking in two thousand twenty-five.

112
00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:27.240
And that shaking corrected the story rather than demolishing it.

113
00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:38.410
In January of two thousand twenty-five, Zachary Compton and colleagues from a large number of institutions combined the widest body of data yet assembled into a single analysis:

114
00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:46.860
sixteen thousand and forty-nine necropsy records, from two hundred and ninety-two species, across ninety-nine animal-care institutions.

115
00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:50.830
The result ran against Peto's forty-year-old observation.

116
00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:55.120
As body mass rose, the rate at which tumours appeared rose with it.

117
00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:02.450
The increase was small, but it was there: when mass went up tenfold, tumour risk climbed by about two point one percent.

118
00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:09.140
The same data threw up another interesting link as well; species with long gestation periods had less cancer.

119
00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:14.390
When gestation went up tenfold, tumour risk fell by five point three percent.

120
00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:20.600
A month later, in February of two thousand twenty-five, a team including George Butler,

121
00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:31.290
Joanna Baker and Chris Venditti published a study of two hundred and sixty-three species in the journal of the United States National Academy of Sciences, and the title did not beat about the bush:

122
00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:35.900
no evidence for Peto's paradox in terrestrial vertebrates.

123
00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:44.030
Among amphibians, birds, mammals and squamate reptiles, large species were getting more cancer than small ones.

124
00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:50.560
Care is needed here, because these two studies are saying something more measured than most of the news coverage suggested.

125
00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.570
Yes, the relationship is not zero.

126
00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:56.260
But look at the size of the relationship.

127
00:11:56.260 --> 00:12:02.630
The naive calculation expects that when mass goes up tenfold, risk goes up roughly tenfold too.

128
00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:05.480
What was measured is two percent.

129
00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:09.610
So the curve is not flat, but it is very nearly flat.

130
00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:14.060
The paradox has not been solved; it has been cut down to size.

131
00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:23.150
Somebody still has to fill the enormous gap in between, and what fills that gap are mechanisms like the elephant's twenty copies and the naked mole rat's hyaluronan.

132
00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:27.600
The second finding from Venditti's team sits exactly in that gap.

133
00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:35.730
Among birds and mammals, the lineages whose body size grew rapidly over evolutionary time were the lineages with the lower cancer rates.

134
00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:42.420
In other words, the faster an animal got big, the more tightly it was forced to clamp down on cell control.

135
00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:45.270
The elephant fits this picture perfectly.

136
00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:48.920
A fast-growing lineage that paid the bill at the till.

137
00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:54.330
It is also only honest to say that the argument is not closed.

138
00:12:54.330 --> 00:13:04.060
In July of two thousand twenty-five, Antoine Dujon and his team reanalysed the same data sets with Bayesian methods and arrived at a more cautious conclusion:

139
00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:09.470
the evidence in hand is not enough either to confirm or to reject the paradox outright.

140
00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:11.840
The reasons are concrete.

141
00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:17.410
All of these records come from zoos, whereas these defence systems evolved in the wild.

142
00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:24.260
Some species have hundreds of necropsy records and others have three or four, which shifts the curve easily.

143
00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:29.990
And diseases with wildly different biology are being tipped into one bag under the heading of cancer.

144
00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:33.960
This is not science collapsing; it is science working.

145
00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:37.610
An observation corrects itself as the data grow.

146
00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.620
So what does all of this tell us?

147
00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:45.230
Not a direct promise of a cure, and let us be clear about that.

148
00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:58.160
Nobody is going to add nineteen TP53 retrogenes to the human genome tomorrow; such an application is entirely speculative today, and nobody knows what a body carrying extra suicide buttons would look like.

149
00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:09.010
But we already hold one piece of evidence, tried at laboratory scale, that did work: when the naked mole rat's gene was transferred into mice, the mice really did get less cancer.

150
00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:14.740
That shows that at least some of the solutions evolution has found are transferable between species.

151
00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:25.590
On the elephant side, researchers are examining the elephant's p53 protein directly as a therapeutic candidate; that work is still at an early stage and is not something proven in the clinic.

152
00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:29.080
What lasts is the change of perspective.

153
00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:36.090
For a long time cancer was regarded as an unavoidable tax; the price of living, of carrying cells, of ageing.

154
00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.940
The elephant's genome changes that.

155
00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:44.190
Cancer risk is not a fixed constant of nature; it is an adjustable parameter.

156
00:14:44.190 --> 00:14:53.600
Evolution has pulled that parameter down independently, again and again: in the elephant by multiplying copies, in the naked mole rat by changing tissue chemistry,

157
00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:57.570
in other lineages by routes we have not yet fully worked out.

158
00:14:57.570 --> 00:15:07.300
A blind process has solved the same problem dozens of times with whatever scrap was to hand, with a stretch of gene that happened to duplicate, with the resurrection of a dead copy.

159
00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:13.350
If something can be solved over and over, then that something belongs to engineering, not to fate.

160
00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:17.800
What sits in the genomes of elephants is precisely this knowledge.

161
00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.290
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

162
00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:24.940
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
